                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "55fd6d6b-0187-3e8d-ad4f-f2de4316b4b2",
                        "entry_id": "51c4d32e-05f6-3d9d-b4b1-e183ba1d8ec9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Rugged, rough, unrefined."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "78466c4f-303e-31bc-8433-37f434b9a8b9",
                        "entry_id": "51c4d32e-05f6-3d9d-b4b1-e183ba1d8ec9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having a rough, wrinkled, or wavy surface; commonly in parasynthetic usage e.g. \\\"rugose-veined\\\" or \\\"rugose-leaved\\\"."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6c232a0e-e05b-4e26-a809-5d43eb0b8d5d",
                                "sense_id": "78466c4f-303e-31bc-8433-37f434b9a8b9",
                                "text": "Petraea foliis rugosis ovatis. The oval, rugose-leaved Petraea. The root is brachiated; the shrub rises to ten feet high: the leaves are three inches long, an inch and a half broad, even at the edges, and very rough to the touch: (Note: In modern nomenclature, the plant in question probably is Petrea rugosa, as the spelling \"Petraea\" does not seem to match any extant genus,)"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "05ef9367-b1af-4e50-9343-67de5075b181",
                                "sense_id": "78466c4f-303e-31bc-8433-37f434b9a8b9",
                                "text": "The original leaves were flat and very rugose, and almost hid by the numerous single-stemmed flowers; while the leaves produced in the umbellate state were larger, more upright, and less wrinkled."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "abe2d9d7-5cd8-3217-adb8-d7b55692eee7",
                        "entry_id": "51c4d32e-05f6-3d9d-b4b1-e183ba1d8ec9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Describing a fossil coral of the extinct order †Rugosa (also called Tetracoralla), this order has horn-shaped corals with surfaces covered with ridges."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "08a4bbcd-76b4-3a14-8d41-b54ad4cecdd2",
                        "entry_id": "51c4d32e-05f6-3d9d-b4b1-e183ba1d8ec9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Used when combined with another adjective, for example, rugose-reticulate or rugose-punctate."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
